 ],
 "literal": "Having pitied, saying 'the soft-natured dark peacock will shiver', / you gave your shawl, O Began of unfading good fame, / with rutting elephants and spirited horses! / We do not come being hungry, nor do we have any burden of family. / Tuning the small lute with a black stem like a ripe kalavu fruit, / making those who dwell desiring melody tremble, / saying 'O you who desire grace, do righteous deeds!' / This is the gift we requested: / mounting your tall chariot with a group of bells, / remove the deep grief of her who dwells in pain in the darkness.",
 "translation": "O Began of unfading good fame, with rutting elephants and spirited horses, you who once pitied a soft-natured peacock, saying 'it will shiver,' and wrapped it in your shawl! We do not come to you because we are hungry, nor do we have the burden of a family to feed. Tuning our small, black-stemmed lute, which resembles a ripe kalavu fruit, we made those who love sweet melody tremble with emotion, and sang: 'O you who desire to show grace, do what is righteous!' This is the only gift we beg of you: mount your tall chariot adorned with a cluster of bells, and go in the darkness to remove the deep grief of your wife, who lives there in painful solitude.",
 "modern": "மடப்பமும் அழகும் உடைய கரிய மயில் குளிரால் நடுங்கும் என்று இரக்கப்பட்டு, அதற்குத் தன் போர்வையை அளித்த அழியாத நல்ல புகழைக் கொண்டவனே! மதயானைகளையும் வேகமான குதிரைகளையும் உடைய பேகனே! நாங்கள் பசியோடு வரவில்லை; எங்களுக்குக் குடும்பப் பாரமும் இல்லை. களாப்பழம் போன்ற கரிய தண்டினைக் கொண்ட சிறிய யாழினை, இசை விரும்பும் மக்கள் நடுங்கும்படி இனிமையாக இசைத்து, 'அருளை விரும்பும் வள்ளலே! அறம் செய்வாயாக!' என்று பாடினோம். யாம் உன்னிடம் இரந்து கேட்கும் பரிசு இதுவே: மணிகள் கட்டப்பட்ட உனது நெடிய தேரில் ஏறி, இந்த இருள் சூழ்ந்த இரவில் சென்று, உன்னைப் பிரிந்து துன்பத்துடன் வாழும் உன் மனைவியின் அரிய துயரத்தைத் தீர்ப்பாயாக!",
 "explanation": "The poet Kapilar addresses the chieftain Vaiyāvik Kōpperum Pēkaṉ, who has abandoned his wife Kaṇṇaki for another woman. Kapilar, speaking on behalf of the bards, tells Pēkaṉ that they have not come to his court seeking wealth or food to relieve their hunger. Instead, they have tuned their small, black-stemmed lutes to play moving melodies, pleading with him to perform a righteous deed. The only 'gift' they request from this famously generous patron—who once famously gave his own shawl to a shivering peacock—is that he return to his grieving wife. By mounting his bell-adorned chariot and rushing back to her in the dark, he can dispel her deep sorrow and restore justice to his household.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
 "generated": "2026-11-26"
}